## 🎯 Overview & Objectives

Session 7 focused on algorithmic problem-solving in Python[cite: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6]. We transitioned from fundamental function declarations to implementing core data structures and algorithms—including divide-and-conquer recursion, linear search variations, and bubble sort mechanics[cite: 1, 2, 3, 4]. Finally, we applied these search, sort, and filter algorithms to real-world dataset schemas involving collections of nested dictionaries[cite: 6, 7, 8].

---

## 🧠 Key Concepts & Learning Summary

### 1. Advanced Recursion & Stack Frames
* **Recursive Maximum Value:** Implemented recursive logic to evaluate array maximums by continuously reducing problem size ($N-1$) until hitting base case length 1[cite: 1].
* **Recursive Array Product:** Analyzed execution call stacks for mathematical series reduction ($O(N)$ space complexity due to function call frames)[cite: 2].

### 2. Search Mechanics
* **Linear Search ($O(N)$):** Traversed collection sequences to evaluate target items[cite: 3]. Applied variadic keyword arguments (`**kwargs`) to build flexible search interfaces[cite: 3].
* **Conditional Filtering:** Filtered arrays using condition predicates to extract specific subsets[cite: 5].

### 3. Sorting Algorithms (Bubble Sort)
* Implemented **Bubble Sort** ($O(N^2)$ time complexity) utilizing nested loops and in-place Pythonic tuple swapping (`a, b = b, a`)[cite: 4].
* Enhanced sorting algorithms to accept direction flags (`low_to_high`) and dynamic attribute lookup keys[cite: 7].

### 4. Applied Data Processing: Flight Reservation Engine
* Modeled domain entities using list-of-dictionary structures (e.g., flight catalogs containing metadata like carrier, fare, duration, and route)[cite: 6].
* Extended search, filter, and sorting modules to operate dynamically over nested data structures[cite: 6, 7, 8].

